// Conformance row C12 — a Copy operand must survive a comparison
// whose conformance consumes `other`
//
// The guarantee this row states is the one every position of DF-216b is about:
// comparing two values does not destroy either of them. `Held` is
// auto-Copy (one `String` field) and its hand-written `equals` does
// `move other`, which its by-value parameter entitles it to. The operator hands
// over a loaded value and adds no retain, so each comparison releases a
// reference nobody took. HOW THIS FAILS IS PLATFORM-DEPENDENT, and the
// Aug-17 refcount-underflow detector does not change that for THIS shape
// (probed): the first over-release finds rc==1 and is indistinguishable at
// the release site from a legitimate last-owner drop — it frees the
// still-referenced storage, and every later release reads FREED memory, so
// the detector sees allocator scribble, not zero. macOS's allocator SIGTRAPs
// after enough iterations; glibc's tolerates it and the row XPASSes on Linux
// hosts — the platform-conditional-marker ruling this row owes is still
// open. The identical body that READS `other` instead of consuming it
// survives (the difference is exactly the `move`).
//
// This is an EIGHTH position, past the seven DF-216b's class sweep probed: the
// sweep tested NoCopy operands throughout, and the stopgap's tier condition
// excludes Copy on the stated grounds that retain semantics make the
// borrow sound. They do not — the retain is not there. Whether the answer is to
// widen the refusal, to retain at the lowering, or to take `other: &Self` (which
// removes the callee's ability to consume at every tier at once) is a ruling,
// so the row pins the PROPERTY rather than any one mechanism.
// XFAIL: DF-216b a hand-written `equals` that consumes `other` over-releases a Copy operand through the operator — the eighth matrix position, outside the stopgap's ExplicitCopy/NoCopy tier condition
// EXPECT: success
// EXPECT-OUTPUT:
// before: a=ALPHA b=BRAVO
// after: a=ALPHA b=BRAVO

struct Held { name: String }

extension Held: Equatable {
    func equals(&self, other: Held) -> Bool {
        let taken = move other
        self.name.len() == taken.name.len()
    }
}

func main() {
    let a = Held(name: "alpha".to_uppercase())
    let b = Held(name: "bravo".to_uppercase())
    print("before: a={a.name} b={b.name}")
    var k = 0
    while k < 200 {
        if a == b { }
        k = k + 1
    }
    print("after: a={a.name} b={b.name}")
}
